Overlay patch 0020 (rev2): byte-budget texture cache for 4K packs (iOS).
rev2: DeleteTexture's ledger subtract is mip-aware (0019 rev3+ gives large
textures a full mip chain = +1/3 bytes over the base level).
The 4K long-session pause crash (device, 2026-07-13): Fast3D's texture
cache is COUNT-based (TEXTURE_CACHE_MAX_SIZE=1024) and the Metal
backend's DeleteTexture was EMPTY — evicted ids park on a free list and
the MTLTexture is only released if that id is later reused with
different dims. With 4K-pack textures up to 8192x8192 (256 MB each,
typical 2048x2048 = 16 MB), a long session pins gigabytes of GPU
memory; the pause menu's Kaleido upload burst then tips the app into
jetsam (silent SIGKILL) or a nil newTexture (crash fixed in 0019 rev2).
Fix, three parts:
1. gfx_rendering_api.h: virtual GetTextureBytes() (default 0 so other
   backends are unaffected and the budget is disabled there).
2. Metal: DeleteTexture actually releases the texture + exact byte
   ledger (created in 0019 rev2's UploadTexture accounting).
3. interpreter.cpp ImportTexture tail (__IOS__): after each upload,
   evict LRU entries until resident bytes <= gSohIos.TexCacheBudgetMB,
   keeping >=128 entries. Draws are flushed before imports and command
   buffers retain in-flight textures, so releasing at evict time is
   GPU-safe — same invariants the count eviction already relies on.
   Rate-limited eviction log for remote logtail.
rev3 (VR R8 part C, the jetsam round): two changes, both because the
Vision Pro's budget is not the iPhone's.
a. THE DEFAULT IS VR-AWARE — 512 MB in VR, 1536 MB everywhere else.
   1536 was tuned on the flat iPhone path, where VR's ~800 MB of eye,
   HUD and compositor surfaces did not exist; on the headset that
   ceiling plus the VR tax plus an unbounded resource cache is the
   4.9 GB jetsam of 2026-09-05. An explicit CVar still wins, which is
   what keeps `cvar gSohIos.TexCacheBudgetMB N` an honest A/B.
b. THE COUNT EVICTION NOW DELETES THE TEXTURE. TextureCacheLookup's
   1024-entry cap parked the evicted id on the free list and left the
   MTLTexture resident until that id was reused with different
   dimensions — so the count cap, the one eviction upstream actually
   has, freed nothing. It calls DeleteTexture now, exactly as the byte
   budget below already does, and the ledger stays truthful.

